504 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 230 0 R
/E (The Development Subcommittee conducted door-to-door surveys over two days in Stung Meanchey and Koh Dach Island. In collaboration with other subcommittees, we developed survey questions covering health demographics, interest in screening, and educational status to ensure each subcommittee could identify needs relevant to its work. As this was our first engagement in these villages, we prioritised collecting quantitative data to keep surveys concise, increase the number of households reached, and obtain more representative and actionable insights.)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
505 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 230 0 R
/E (Our subcommittee employed field-based convenience sampling to conduct surveys within the villages. While this approach introduced limitations such as selection bias for households of poorer living conditions as well as an overrepresentation of residents who were available at home during weekdays, it was chosen for its practicality and efficiency. As an initial assessment of these communities, convenience sampling enabled rapid data collection without requiring prior knowledge of the area, while also capturing the demographic most likely to attend future screening programmes.)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
