515 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 231 0 R
/E (Residents demonstrated strong interest in health screening programmes and follow-up care. However, willingness to participate appeared closely tied to accessibility and affordability. Many residents preferred screening locations close to home, while a significant proportion lacked healthcare subsidy coverage. These findings suggest that community-based screening programmes may receive strong uptake if they are conducted in accessible locations. Clear follow-up pathways would also be important to ensure that screening translates into meaningful care.)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
516 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 231 0 R
/E (Health)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
517 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 231 0 R
/E (Most households reported relying on bottled water provided by the government, suggesting that water procurement may not be a major concern for many residents. However, the community still faced a notable burden of acute illnesses, non-communicable diseases such as hypertension and diabetes, and ongoing risks from mosquito-borne diseases. These findings point towards the need for both health screening and preventive health education. Future initiatives could focus on common community health concerns, including chronic disease awareness, mosquito-borne disease prevention and health-seeking behaviour.)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
