/P 248 0 R
/E (First, the Singapore committee had limited visibility over the scholar selection process. While local partners remained central to identifying students in need, the lack of a standardised selection structure made it difficult for Project Battambang to understand how scholars were chosen each year.)
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
628 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 248 0 R
/E (Second, the funding system became increasingly difficult to sustain. As LADS funds were drawn from the general committee pool, it was harder to track how much money was specifically donated for scholarships. It also meant that LADS relied heavily on broader project funds. With changes in fundraising patterns and donor support, this raised concerns about whether the programme could continue supporting scholars at the same scale in the long run.)
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
629 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 248 0 R
/E (Third, follow-up with scholars became more challenging. In the past, LADS Camp provided a regular opportunity for Project Battambang to reconnect with scholars, understand their progress and maintain a relationship with them. However, with current safety concerns preventing regular trips back to Poipet, the team recognised the need for a new system of engagement. Without stronger follow-up, there is a risk that the link between scholars, sponsors and Project Battambang may weaken over time.)
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
